    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Carlotta was named after Carlotta Vance, the daughter of John M. Vance, who laid out the town as a summer resort.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carlotta%2C_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Carlotta Hotel, a three‑story wooden resort built in 1903, was nominated to the National Register of Historic Places for its unchanged architecture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carlotta%2C_California",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During the 1930s, a logging boom brought two large mills and worker housing, reshaping Carlotta from a quiet resort into a bustling lumber town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carlotta%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town sits 6.5 miles southeast of Fortuna, just east of US Route 101 on California State Route 36, and lies beside Yager Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carlotta%2C_California",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Carlotta’s first post office opened in 1903, and the town’s early community included a hotel, store, blacksmith, and saloon.",
